  25 ## New problems found in revision 2 (fixed in revision 3)
  26 
  27 1. The event-shuffled arm permuted pulses across the whole run, so per-block pulse counts could change, contrary to its own description.
  28 2. Unknown-verification trials were not said to skip the diary and replay.
  29 3. Sawtooth checkpoints were not said to be taken before the replay and scored without the diary.
  30 4. The all-legal measure's boards and the learners' tuning block were not named for every stage.
  31 
  32 ## Strongest remaining hypothesis and its rival
  33 
  34 - **Strongest hypothesis:** if the fly can acquire a chess idea at all, interleaved replay of its own lessons will keep that idea while a second idea is learned, beyond extra practice, timing and re-exposure.
  35 - **Credible rival:** replay keeps old ideas by refreshing a case classifier in the connections, and the fly's apparent combinations are case recall not covered by the listed controls.
  36 - **What separates them:** on stage 3's new kinds of problem and unseen combinations, the twin-pair design makes similar-board recall give both twins the same answer. The rival predicts the fly scores near the full-history case-recall and random-feature controls there; the hypothesis predicts it beats them by the protocol's margin.
  37 
  38 ## Counterexample check
  39 
  40 A run could pass stages 1 and 2 while the fly holds only a refreshed case table: both stages test retention on development twins of the practised kinds, which case recall in the weights can serve. That is why stages 1 and 2 support only **assisted retention**, and any reasoning wording waits for stage 3 against the case-recall and learner arms.
  41 
  42 ## Second read, before the reviewers' round 2 (revision 5)
  43 
  44 While the reviewers waited for the usage limit to reset, the lead read revision 4 again as a hostile reviewer and fixed six gaps, listed in section 12 of the design. The most important: section 7.2 claimed identical online lessons for all arms while letting stage 1 arms choose for themselves, so the stage 1 controls would have seen different lessons. Every added stage 1 arm is now yoked to the diary arm's choices. Revision 4 is preserved as [design-revision-4.md](design-revision-4.md) (blob `99df41e4`); revision 5 is blob `0c71bb4d`. The reviewers' round 2 prompts now point at revision 5 and list every edit since revision 2.
  45 
  46 ## Solo deep-in-thought pass (revision 6)
  47 
  48 The researcher asked for Problem Solving at deep-in-thought, solo, while the reviewers wait. This is that pass. It is not independent.
  49 
  50 **Numbers rebuilt from raw records.**
  51 
  52 1. Cubic-exit run (`runs/memory-retention-2026-11-01/eye-graph-memory-cubic-v1-recovery1/records.jsonl`): all five original marks pass on six-seed medians (delay ratio 0.7199). Per seed, the delay ratios are 0.7566, 0.5941, 0.6832, 0.8118, 0.8714 and 0.4203. After the reversal, seeds 4102, 4105 and 4106 each lose one unrelated association; no seed loses an old item at the end of learning. Seed 4102's item 3 margin goes from 0.140130 to -0.020325, matching the design's quote. The run took 6,157 s at 299 MiB peak.
  53 2. v8 (`summary.json`): full store 349.33, piece-value rule 294.67, all-legal 30.60 against 21.92.
  54 3. Eyes v2 input size, graph-free, 83 moves on three boards: 1,644 active photoreceptors on average, at most 1,955, so 13-16 KiB per diary entry.
  55 4. Stage 2 work: about 72,000 forward passes per run, 216,000 for three runs, about 22.5 hours at 0.375 s per pass. Revision 5 understated this as 15-20 hours.
  56 5. Chance of 32 or more of 48 pairs by guessing: 1.47%.
  57 
  58 **Three critique briefs, run by the lead.**
  59 
  60 - *Peer (could two teams compute the same verdict?):* bootstrap seed, lower-limit definition and tie tolerance were missing; development boards were not said to be disjoint from the tape; runs that stop early had no rule. All specified in revision 6, with a worked example at the exact cutoffs.
  61 - *Scientific skeptic (could another mechanism give the same result?):* the filler, no-pulse and event-shuffled controls changed replays during the A block, so they would learn A less well, and the diary could beat them on learning rather than keeping. Revision 6 makes every replay control change only old-lesson replays, so each starts B in exactly the diary arm's state.
  62 - *Foundational (does a pass answer the question?):* stage 1 asked the diary to stop delay losses that occur with no lessons and so no replays, which is outside its mechanism; a working diary would have failed. Revision 6 scopes the delay as "not worse", and counts benefits only after new learning. The diary's link to reasoning is now stated: it keeps earlier ideas alive so the fly can combine them; the combining is the fly's.
