**VERDICT: APPROVE WITH REQUIRED CHANGES.** Reviewed revision 7, blob `8d1fc4f2…`. Building requires the required-BUILD correction below.

**Round-1 findings**

1. **RESOLVED:** delivered-input identity removes the FEN-counter path; the interface exposes no move-scoring query.
2. **RESOLVED for the design:** the lesson boundary separates teacher evidence, simultaneous neural delivery and audited receipts.
3. **RESOLVED for the design:** stage 1 now declares its older learner, injected codes, complete state and revised per-seed gates.
4. **RESOLVED:** explicit tapes and old-lesson replay controls replace the previously mismatched comparisons.
5. **STILL OPEN before launch:** game eligibility manifests and frozen executable specifications remain prospective; the revised cross-board tie rule also remains ambiguous.

**New findings**

1. **required-BUILD — Supersession contradicts the promised replay dose.**  
   [Admission removes matching entries before replay selection](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:51), while [section 4.3 requires exactly one replay except after the first lesson](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:182). If the first two lessons have identical input, admission removes the first entry; only the current entry remains, so replay returns nothing. This changes dose and control matching.  
   **Smallest fix:** specify “at most one, when an older entry remains,” and match controls to actual replay events. Also qualify capacity pressure: stage 1 has at most eight distinct codes but 105 teaching events, giving capacity 27; supersession therefore prevents eviction pressure at one quarter.

2. **required-LAUNCH — The updated control rules still yield contradictory interpretations.**  
   [Stage 2 requires forgetting in “no old replays”](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:319), but [section 8 stops the claim when “no diary” keeps the earlier idea](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:359). Those outcomes can coexist, producing opposite verdicts. Further, the [moved-timing arm](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:271) deliberately receives extra replays before the next block, contradicting the claim that all five controls start it in the diary’s exact state.  
   **Smallest fix:** propagate the new forgetting reference into section 8. Restrict the identical-state claim to the applicable controls and first common snapshot; later stage-1 blocks already have different learning histories.

3. **required-LAUNCH — Twin-pair tie handling has two possible tolerances.**  
   [Section 7.4 defines tolerance from the largest score “on that board”](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:313), but a pair compares two boards. With board maxima 1 and 0.1, their tolerances are `1.01e−7` and `1.1e−8`. A score difference of `5e−8` is a tie under one and correct under the other. This can change acquisition, retention and transfer verdicts.  
   **Smallest fix:** define one pair tolerance—such as the maximum of both board tolerances—and explicitly define its application to offset-cancelled margins. Add this boundary example to the frozen evaluator.

4. **optional — The revised runtime estimate uses a partial measurement as a whole-run measurement.**  
   [Stage 1 calls 6,157 seconds the duration of 24 arm runs](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:289), whereas the [source explicitly labels timing and RSS as recovery-invocation measurements](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/memory-retention-2026-11-01/eye-graph-memory-cubic-v1-recovery1/summary.json:136).  
   **Smallest fix:** correct the measurement’s scope and leave the extrapolated duration unverified until the dry run.

**Strongest supportable sentence, conditional on a valid pass:** “The fly trained with a disclosed replay diary retained earlier learning and outperformed the listed controls on unfamiliar combinations of three separately taught chess ideas; untested case classifiers remain possible.”
